// ADR 0016, register D43: a generic fn whose type parameter appears ONLY in its return
// type. There is no argument to infer from, so the expected type is the only source.
//
// `fn mk<T>() -> ?T { null }` under `let x: ?i64 = mk();`: the oracle seeds T from the
// expectation and emits `@mk__i64`. `scg` had nothing to bind T with and, before the
// seed landed, DIED — `sentinel: index out of bounds: idx=-101, len=4`, exit 127, zero
// bytes emitted, on a program the oracle accepts and `llvm-as` accepts. That abort is
// register D58 and is separately guarded now; this fixture pins the actual inference.
//
// ⚠ THE RETURN TYPE IS `?T`, NOT `T`, ON PURPOSE. This is the discriminator for how the
// seed is gated. The oracle's comment claims it seeds only when the return type "is
// exactly a TypeParam"; its code just unifies. A mirror written from the comment would
// refuse to seed here — the return is a NULLABLE whose inner is the type param — and T
// would stay unbound, which is exactly the state that used to kill the compiler.
//
// ⚠ Its sibling `c19_generic_return_seed_secret.sentinel` pins the `-> T` shape. Keep
// both: a gate written from the comment passes that one and fails this one.
//
// 42 = the nullable came back empty, as `mk` returns `null`.
fn mk<T>() -> ?T {
    null
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    let x: ?i64 = mk();
    if is_some(x) { 1 } else { 42 }
}
